From b897984f24cd68df845be7a00fd44ede9aa63559 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeepSeek Harness <noreply@deepseek.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 00:37:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 05/18] Start the Windows core: transport, signing,
 credentials, QR

The stub in core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp answered all 35 entry points with
{"event":"unsupported"}. This adds the foundation the real implementations
stand on, ported from core/Sources/LiveHimeCore and documented in
docs/port-spec/CONTRACT.md:

  platform/dispatch   DispatchQueue.main, as a message-only window
  net/http            URLSession, on WinHTTP with a 4-worker pool
  net/cookies         HTTPCookieStorage, RFC 6265 domain/path matching
  net/crypto          CryptoKit, on BCrypt
  net/signing         WBISigner and the pc_link app signature
  auth/credentials    the Keychain item, in Credential Manager

Behaviour comes from the Swift, not from a reasonable guess at what it meant:
the 64-entry WBI mixin table and the !'()* filter are copied verbatim, and the
startLive signature keeps the eight fields in the order BilibiliLiveClient.swift
calls load-bearing.

qrcodegen is vendored for the login QR because Windows has no
CIQRCodeGenerator. The header is byte-identical to the copy obs-deps already
ships for the macOS build, so both platforms encode the same symbols; the PNG
is written here through zlib, with the geometry PlatformSupport.swift uses
(error correction M, four-module quiet zone, whole-number scale off 480).

Credential Manager caps a blob at CRED_MAX_CREDENTIAL_BLOB_SIZE (2560) and a
Bilibili session is not length-bounded, so auth/credentials writes a
DPAPI-protected file under %LOCALAPPDATA% when the vault refuses one, and Load
checks both.

Three module bodies are still being written by other agents: live/liveclient,
auth/session + auth/qrlogin, and live/danmaku. The stub is out of the build but
left in the tree as the record of what the first Windows build compiled against.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
